From 0ceee8d9931bea5dee319233adaf1c9fd3098bf3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 12:15:05 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v1] Plan CHECK and generation expressions once per relcache
 entry

ExecRelCheck() reads every CHECK constraint's ccbin with stringToNode(),
and ExecInitGenerated() every generation expression with
build_column_default(), and both run the result through
ExecPrepareExpr() in every statement that needs them.
ExecPrepareExpr() calls expression_planner(), which calls any IMMUTABLE
function whose arguments are constants and copies its result into the
tree.  So a CHECK constraint or a stored generated column that reads
such a function calls it, and copies its result, in every INSERT or
UPDATE, and a CHECK constraint that holds a large constant parses it
back from ccbin each time.  Index expressions, by contrast, are planned
once per relcache entry (RelationGetIndexExpressions()), and domain
constraints once per backend (load_domaintype_info()).

Do the same for CHECK constraints and generation expressions.
RelationGetPlannedCheckExprs(), RelationGetGenerationExpr() and
RelationGetPlannedGenerationExpr() parse, expand and plan them on first
use and keep the result in a memory context the relcache entry owns,
and the two executor sites only run ExecInitExpr() on it, which does
not modify its input.  Generation expressions are planned one column at
a time, as the executor asks for them, so an UPDATE still never plans a
column it does not recompute.

The planned trees depend on the relation's constraints and defaults,
and a relcache rebuild that changes its tupledesc discards them.  They
also depend on what plancache.c revalidates a plan against, none of
which invalidates the relcache: the functions planning called or
inlined (CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION), any domain whose coercion
planning removed because the domain had no constraints (ALTER DOMAIN
... ADD CONSTRAINT), the EXECUTE privilege that calling a function at
plan time checks (GRANT, REVOKE, role membership, SET ROLE, SECURITY
DEFINER), and, where inlining parsed a SQL function's string body, the
search_path and the operators and namespaces it found.  So the entry
records the user and the search_path it planned under, and syscache
callbacks on pg_proc, pg_type, pg_operator, pg_namespace, pg_authid and
pg_auth_members advance a counter that makes every entry's planned
expressions stale, to be planned again on next use.

Executor state points into the cached trees rather than into copies,
so that no constant is copied per statement.  When an open relation's
entry is rebuilt, or its planned expressions go stale, their memory
therefore lives on, handed to the rebuilt entry or kept as a child of
their successor's, until RelationClose() finds the relation
unreferenced, as rd_pdcxt does for partition descriptors.  To bound
what one statement can keep that way, the expressions are started over
at most once while the relation stays open; after that they are planned
in the caller's memory, as before, until it is closed.

On master, one-row INSERTs in a PL/pgSQL loop, each its own statement,
take about 1.6 us of backend CPU time each against a bare table, 28 us
against a table with one CHECK constraint over a SQL function that
returns a 128 kB bit string, 27 us with a stored generated column over
that function instead, and 2.4 ms with a CHECK constraint that holds
the 128 kB constant as a literal.  With this patch the last three take
2.7 us, 3.6 us and 1.8 us.

The new regression test relcache_exprs covers CHECK constraints and
generated columns over constant functions across ADD and DROP
CONSTRAINT and SET EXPRESSION, partitions and inheritance, COPY,
INSERT ... ON CONFLICT, MERGE and UPDATE, a function replaced in place,
a domain constraint added later, EXECUTE granted and revoked, a changed
search_path, executor state outliving an invalidation within its
statement, and that the function is called once rather than once per
statement.  Its output on an unpatched server differs only in that
last line.
